5†v1 ), last revised 14 Nov 2025 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:VoiceAgentEval: A Dual-Dimensional Benchmark for Expert-Level Intelligent Voice-Agent Evaluation of Xbench's Professional-Aligned Series\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Pengyu Xu , cite7†Shijia Li , cite8†Ao Sun , cite9†Feng Zhang , cite10†Yahan Li , cite11†Bo Wu , cite12†Zhanyu Ma , cite13†Jiguo Li , cite14†Jun Xu , cite15†Jiuchong Gao , cite16†Jinghua Hao , cite17†Renqing He , cite18†Rui Wang , cite19†Yang Liu , cite20†Xiaobo Hu , cite21†Fan Yang , cite22†Jia Zheng , cite23†Guanghua Yao L12: View a PDF of the paper titled VoiceAgentEval: A Dual-Dimensional Benchmark for Expert-Level Intelligent Voice-Agent Evaluation of Xbench's Professional-Aligned Series, by Pengyu Xu and 17 other authors\nL13: \nL14: cite24†View PDF cite25†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:We propose OutboundEval, a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating large language models (LLMs) in expert-level intelligent outbound calling scenarios. Unlike existing methods that suffer from three key limitations - insufficient dataset diversity and category coverage, unrealistic user simulation, and inaccurate evaluation metrics - OutboundEval addresses these issues through a structured framework.\nL16: First, we design a benchmark spanning six major business domains and 30 representative sub-scenarios, each with scenario-specific process decomposition, weighted scoring, and domain-adaptive metrics. Second, we develop a large-model-driven User Simulator that generates diverse, persona-rich virtual users with realistic behaviors, emotional variability, and communication styles, providing a controlled yet authentic testing environment.\nL17: Third, we introduce a dynamic evaluation method that adapts to task variations, integrating automated and human-in-the-loop assessment to measure task execution accuracy, professional knowledge application, adaptability, and user experience quality. Experiments on 12 state-of-the-art LLMs reveal distinct trade-offs between expert-level task completion and interaction fluency, offering practical insights for building reliable, human-like outbound AI systems.\nL18: OutboundEval establishes a practical, extensible, and domain-oriented standard for benchmarking LLMs in professional applications.\nL19: Subjects:  | Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite26†arXiv:2510.21244 [cs.AI]\nL21:    | (or cite27†arXiv:2510.21244v2 [cs.AI] for this version)\nL22:    | cite28†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.21244†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Zhanyu Ma [cite29†view email ]\nL26: cite5†[v1] Fri, 24 Oct 2025 08:27:58 UTC (8,358 KB)\nL27: [v2] Fri, 14 Nov 2025 11:59:53 UTC (9,234 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled VoiceAgentEval: A Dual-Dimensional Benchmark for Expert-Level Intelligent Voice-Agent Evaluation of Xbench's Professional-Aligned Series, by Pengyu Xu and 17 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite24†View PDF L36:   * cite25†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite30†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite31†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.AI\nL43: \nL44: cite32†< prev |   cite33†next > L45: \nL46: cite34†new | cite35†recent | cite36†2025-10 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite37†cs L51: \nL52: ### References & Citations\nL53: \nL54:   * cite38†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L55:   * cite39†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L56:   * cite40†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L57: \nL58: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL59: \nL60: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL61: \nL62: [Button: ×]\nL63: \nL64: loading...\nL65: \nL66: Data provided by:\nL67: \nL68: ### Bookmark\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL71: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL72: \nL73: [Input] Bibliographic Explore