 2025 (cite5†v1 ), last revised 14 Nov 2025 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:SWE-Bench Pro: Can AI Agents Solve Long-Horizon Software Engineering Tasks?\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Xiang Deng , cite7†Jeff Da , cite8†Edwin Pan , cite9†Yannis Yiming He , cite10†Charles Ide , cite11†Kanak Garg , cite12†Niklas Lauffer , cite13†Andrew Park , cite14†Nitin Pasari , cite15†Chetan Rane , cite16†Karmini Sampath , cite17†Maya Krishnan , cite18†Srivatsa Kundurthy , cite19†Sean Hendryx , cite20†Zifan Wang , cite21†Vijay Bharadwaj , cite22†Jeff Holm , cite23†Raja Aluri , cite24†Chen Bo Calvin Zhang , cite25†Noah Jacobson , cite26†Bing Liu , cite27†Brad Kenstler L12: View a PDF of the paper titled SWE-Bench Pro: Can AI Agents Solve Long-Horizon Software Engineering Tasks?, by Xiang Deng and 21 other authors\nL13: \nL14: cite28†View PDF cite29†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:We introduce SWE-Bench Pro, a substantially more challenging benchmark that builds upon the best practices of SWE-BENCH [25], but is explicitly designed to capture realistic, complex, enterprise-level problems beyond the scope of SWE-BENCH. SWE-BENCH PRO contains 1,865 problems sourced from a diverse set of 41 actively maintained repositories spanning business applications, B2B services, and developer tools.\nL16: The benchmark is partitioned into a public set with open access to problems sourced from 11 repositories, a held-out set of 12 repositories and a commercial set of 18 proprietary repositories where we have formal partnership agreements with early-stage startups. Problems in the held-out and the commercial set are not publicly accessible, but we release results on the commercial set.\nL17: Our benchmark features long-horizon tasks that may require hours to days for a professional software engineer to complete, often involving patches across multiple files and substantial code modifications. All tasks are human-verified and augmented with sufficient context to ensure resolvability. To better understand these limitations, we cluster the failure modes observed in the collected agent trajectories for a clearer characterization of the error patterns exhibited by current models.\nL18: Overall, SWE-BENCH PRO provides a contamination-resistant testbed that more faithfully captures the complexity and diversity of real-world software development, advancing the pursuit of truly autonomous software engineering agents at a professional level.\nL19: Subjects:  | Software Engineering (cs.SE); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite30†arXiv:2509.16941 [cs.SE]\nL21:    | (or cite31†arXiv:2509.16941v2 [cs.SE] for this version)\nL22:    | cite32†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.16941†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Jeff Da [cite33†view email ]\nL26: cite5†[v1] Sun, 21 Sep 2025 06:28:17 UTC (2,099 KB)\nL27: [v2] Fri, 14 Nov 2025 22:00:03 UTC (1,390 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled SWE-Bench Pro: Can AI Agents Solve Long-Horizon Software Engineering Tasks?, by Xiang Deng and 21 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite28†View PDF L36:   * cite29†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite34†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite35†view license†creativecommons.org L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.SE\nL43: \nL44: cite36†< prev |   cite37†next > L45: \nL46: cite38†new | cite39†recent | cite40†2025-09 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite41†cs L51: cite42†cs.CL L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite43†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite44†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite45†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: 